# Universe content · the grand tour: one flight from the whole visible universe down to the quarks, for watching
# Run it: Universe.exe --tour grand-tour (or Universe.exe --tour grand-tour). A click, the wheel or a key pauses it; T carries on, R starts again.
# Edit the stops to make your own. Each stop: do (shot, zoom, orbit, look, arc, fly, glide, pick) and its numbers. A shot
# frames what is picked by its own size: to-radii (how many of its sizes away) or to-km, sweep (degrees round it), rise
# (degrees up or down), seconds, max-rate (tenfolds of scale a second at most: never streaming past, and never leaving what
# was in view where the shot began faster than that either, so it pulls away and shrinks smoothly);
# through: yes flies on into the next stop without slowing or pausing; pan: degrees a second a zoom circles what it flies to
# (turn: degrees a second for the circling stops). There are no pauses: each caption stays on while the flight goes on.
# A caption of {sky} is written from what is really up from where you stand (its star only if it is in view, its moons, the
# other worlds, the galaxy's stars by night or the day sky by day); {seen} is how much of the universe the tour reached (the
# places it went to against every galaxy, star, world and moon there is, as a true percentage); {hole} from the black hole picked (its jets if it fires
# them, its disc, or quiet; its horizon's size and its mass). The far-galaxy pick prefers a galaxy whose heart is feeding, and
# the black-hole pick a feeding one, so the tour flies by a feeding black hole: its disc, the light it bends, its shadow.
# The film's grammar: an establishing shot; a straight push in or pull back to change scale; a calm circle only round what
# fills the view; looking round the sky from the ground slow (a few degrees a second at most, the sky turning too); a pan where it stands across what is vast; every move eased; circling 6 to 12 degrees a second; scale changing
# a third to a half of a tenfold a second; each shot 6 to 10 seconds. The director varies each shot every viewing.

@stop start
do: pick
target: start-galaxy
caption: Where we begin

@stop open
do: shot
to-radii: 6
sweep: 70
rise: 15
seconds: 5
caption: We begin beside a galaxy, a hundred billion stars turning in the dark

@stop leave
do: shot
to-km: 5e21
rise: 20
max-rate: 0.35
seconds: 5
caption: Pulling back: every point of light out here is a whole galaxy; the far ones redden, their light stretched by the space it crossed

@stop web
do: orbit
turn: 4
seconds: 6
caption: The cosmic web, shaped by dark matter's gravity: galaxies along filaments and walls, round vast thin voids. {horizon}

@stop choose-galaxy
do: pick
target: far-galaxy
far-ly: 1e9..5e9
caption: A far galaxy

@stop cross
do: shot
to-radii: 8
sweep: 20
rise: 10
max-rate: 0.45
seconds: 5
caption: Across the universe: billions of light-years, straight to one galaxy far away among a hundred billion

@stop round-galaxy
do: shot
to-radii: 3
sweep: 100
rise: -20
seconds: 6
caption: A hundred billion stars round a giant black hole

@stop bh
do: pick
target: black-hole
caption: A black hole

@stop to-bh
do: shot
to-radii: 60
sweep: 30
rise: 10
max-rate: 0.5
seconds: 5
caption: Down to the black hole at its heart, one that is feeding

@stop disc
do: shot
to-radii: 10
sweep: 60
rise: 40
max-rate: 0.4
seconds: 6
caption: Its accretion disc: gas on its own orbits, the inner ones round fastest, sheared into spirals; white-hot inside, red at the rim; stars swept round it on tight ellipses

@stop skim
do: shot
to-radii: 14
sweep: 150
rise: 3
max-rate: 0.3
seconds: 7
caption: Skimming it in the plane of its disc: a feeding one's gas at a good share of light speed, brighter on the side coming at you, a star torn into a stream and swallowed; a quiet one a black disc in its ring of bent light

@stop lens
do: shot
to-radii: 4
sweep: 90
rise: -45
max-rate: 0.4
seconds: 6
caption: Edge on, its gravity bends the light behind it into a thin ring round its shadow (the photon ring), and a feeding one's disc's far side up and over it

@stop horizon
do: shot
to-radii: 2.5
sweep: 50
rise: 10
max-rate: 0.35
seconds: 5
caption: The shadow, two and a half times the horizon across: light itself is caught here, and nothing inside gets out

@stop to-horizon
do: shot
to-radii: 0.45
sweep: 140
rise: -25
max-rate: 0.25
seconds: 10
caption: Down to the horizon itself: the disc all round, the sky bent into a ring, the shadow growing to fill the view; the well's graph marks the way down

@stop fall
do: zoom
rate: 2.2
until-km: 1e-19
through: yes
caption: In through the horizon, looking back at it: the well itself round you, space's sheet bent into a funnel and, inside the throat, a cone, every line running down to the point; the way out needs more than light's speed; the view turns over as you fall, to face the point near the end

@stop singularity
do: orbit
turn: 6
seconds: 5
caption: The singularity: its whole mass in a point with no size at all, as far as the laws we have can say; quarks pressed closer than anything, until those laws give out

@stop out-of-hole
do: zoom
rate: -2.2
until-km: 1e40
out: yes
caption: Back out past the horizon (the one way nothing real can go)

@stop hole-back
do: shot
to-radii: 200
sweep: 40
rise: 25
max-rate: 0.5
seconds: 5
caption: {hole}

@stop supernova
do: pick
target: supernova
caption: A star dying: a supernova

@stop to-supernova
do: shot
to-radii: 10
sweep: 30
rise: 10
max-rate: 0.6
seconds: 6
caption: {supernova}

@stop round-supernova
do: shot
to-radii: 5
sweep: 100
rise: 20
max-rate: 0.4
seconds: 5
caption: Brighter than a hundred million stars for weeks: its light fades over months, its shell flies on for a hundred thousand years, and what its core became stays

@stop choose-star
do: pick
target: star
caption: A star

@stop to-star
do: shot
to-km: 2e9
sweep: 20
max-rate: 0.5
seconds: 5
caption: Out to one star among them

@stop onto-star
do: zoom
rate: 0.6
until-km: 1
caption: Onto its face: the photosphere, the plasma its light comes from

@stop into-star
do: zoom
rate: 0.5
until-km: 1e-12
through: yes
caption: Into the plasma: too hot for atoms to hold their electrons, bare nuclei with free electrons racing between them

@stop star-choose-nucleus
do: pick
target: nucleus
caption: The nucleus

@stop star-to-nucleus
do: zoom
rate: 0.45
until-km: 3e-17
caption: Inward: an atom is almost all empty space

@stop star-round-nucleus
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 24
seconds: 5
caption: Its nucleus: protons and neutrons bound by the strong force

@stop star-choose-proton
do: pick
target: proton
caption: A proton

@stop star-to-proton
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 2.5e-18
caption: Into a single proton

@stop star-round-proton
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 20
seconds: 4
caption: Three quarks in a storm of gluons

@stop star-choose-quark
do: pick
target: quark
caption: A quark

@stop star-to-quark
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 6e-19
caption: One quark

@stop star-at-quark
do: orbit
turn: 8
seconds: 4
caption: The smallest thing we know

@stop star-let-go
do: pick
target: none
caption: And back out

@stop out-of-star
do: zoom
rate: -0.7
until-km: 1e-9
caption: Back out through the plasma to its face

@stop off-star
do: zoom
rate: -0.7
until-km: 3e6
caption: Off its face, out among its worlds

@stop round-system
do: shot
to-km: 8e8
sweep: 100
rise: 30
seconds: 6
caption: The star, and the worlds born round it, each on its own tilted orbit

@stop asteroid
do: pick
target: asteroid
caption: An asteroid

@stop to-asteroid
do: shot
to-radii: 6
sweep: 20
max-rate: 0.5
seconds: 7
caption: Out to its belt of rubble

@stop round-asteroid
do: shot
to-radii: 3
sweep: 120
rise: 15
seconds: 5
caption: {body}

@stop comet
do: pick
target: comet
caption: A comet

@stop to-comet
do: shot
to-km: 4e5
sweep: 30
rise: 10
max-rate: 0.5
seconds: 5
caption: A comet falling past its star, its tails streaming away from it

@stop near-comet
do: shot
to-radii: 15
sweep: 90
rise: -10
seconds: 5
caption: Into its coma: gas and dust boiling off a dark nucleus of ice

@stop onto-comet
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 0.002
caption: Onto its surface: its own ground, of its own kind's minerals (the card says which); down to where a person would stand

@stop comet-stand
do: pick
target: none
caption: Standing

@stop comet-look-up
do: look
tilt: 55
turn: 1.5
seconds: 4
caption: Look up: {sky}

@stop comet-sky
do: orbit
turn: 3
seconds: 4
caption: {sky}

@stop comet-look-down
do: look
tilt: -85
turn: 1.5
seconds: 3
caption: And down: there is as much below us as above

@stop comet-to-grains
do: zoom
rate: 0.4
until-km: 2e-6
through: yes
caption: The ground breaks into stones, then grains of sand

@stop comet-choose-grain
do: pick
target: grain
caption: One grain of sand

@stop comet-to-face
do: zoom
rate: 0.45
until-km: 2e-9
through: yes
caption: Onto one grain, a crystal of one mineral

@stop comet-choose-spot
do: pick
target: spot
caption: A spot on its face

@stop comet-to-crystal
do: zoom
rate: 0.5
until-km: 6e-12
caption: Down to its atoms, in a pattern repeated billions of times

@stop comet-round-crystal
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 15
seconds: 5
caption: A full turn among the atoms of the crystal: its lattice repeated billions of times, the same on every side of you

@stop comet-into-lattice
do: arc
rate: 0.25
until-km: 1.5e-12
turn: 6
seconds: 5
caption: Down among them

@stop comet-through-lattice
do: fly
rate: 0.6
turn: 3
seconds: 5
caption: Through the lattice: row on row of atoms, each bonded to its neighbours

@stop comet-choose-atom
do: pick
target: atom
caption: One atom

@stop comet-to-atom
do: zoom
rate: 0.4
until-km: 6e-13
caption: An atom: its nucleus, and its electrons where they may be

@stop comet-round-atom
do: arc
rate: 0.03
until-km: 2.5e-13
turn: 14
seconds: 6
caption: Round the atom: a tiny nucleus, its electrons round it, each in its own orbital's shape, the paths slowly turning

@stop comet-through-electrons
do: zoom
rate: 0.3
until-km: 5e-14
pan: 12
caption: In among its electrons: each a point on its path; the nucleus a spark at the middle, a hundred thousand times smaller than the atom

@stop comet-inner-shells
do: orbit
turn: 15
seconds: 5
caption: A full turn through the inner shells, held tightest and moving fastest: an atom is almost all empty space, and the same behind you as ahead

@stop comet-to-neighbour
do: pick
target: bonded-atom
caption: Its neighbour

@stop comet-across-bond
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 8
seconds: 4
caption: Across the bond to its neighbour: atoms share their outer electrons

@stop comet-choose-electron
do: pick
target: electron
caption: One electron

@stop comet-to-electron
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 2.5e-14
pan: 8
caption: In to one electron, as to a comet: it has no size anyone has found, so this glow is the size we give it; the nucleus a spark far off

@stop comet-follow-electron
do: orbit
turn: 12
seconds: 5
caption: Riding beside it as it goes round, the view circling: a new path every time round, as an electron has no path of its own, only a cloud of where it may be

@stop comet-choose-nucleus
do: pick
target: nucleus
caption: The nucleus

@stop comet-to-nucleus
do: zoom
rate: 0.45
until-km: 3e-17
caption: Inward: an atom is almost all empty space

@stop comet-round-nucleus
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 24
seconds: 5
caption: Its nucleus: protons and neutrons bound by the strong force

@stop comet-choose-proton
do: pick
target: proton
caption: A proton

@stop comet-to-proton
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 2.5e-18
caption: Into a single proton

@stop comet-round-proton
do: arc
rate: 0
until-km: 0
turn: 20
seconds: 4
caption: Three quarks in a storm of gluons

@stop comet-choose-quark
do: pick
target: quark
caption: A quark

@stop comet-to-quark
do: zoom
rate: 0.35
until-km: 6e-19
caption: One quark

@stop comet-at-quark
do: orbit
turn: 8
seconds: 4
caption: The smallest thing we know

@stop comet-let-go
do: pick
target: none
caption: And back out

@stop comet-out-of-atom
do: zoom
rate: -0.7
until-km: 1e-9
caption: Back out through the atom, the crystal, the grain

@stop off-comet
do: zoom
rate: -0.7
until-km: 1e40
out: yes
caption: Back out to its surface, and its sky

@stop giant
